#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Setup {#setup}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The helper module "
"[`mpo_learning.jl`](https://github.com/Qiskit/documentation/blob/main/docs/tutorials/assets/classical-"
"shadows/julia/mpo_learning.jl), which the Setup cell downloads, implements "
"the learner. The tutorial calls the following functions from it:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Function | Purpose |\n"
"| --- | --- |\n"
"| `kicked_ising_reference(N, D; depolarization)` | Exact MPO of the ideal kicked-Ising state, or of the state after local depolarizing noise |\n"
"| `split_settings(group; seed, train_fraction)` | Randomly assign measurement settings to training and testing sets (half each by default) |\n"
"| `training_marginals(train; ell, prior)` | Shadow estimates of every learning window, from the training settings only |\n"
"| `fitted_prior(ideal, train)` | Prior for common randomized measurements (CRM), fitted to the measured purity of each window, as in the paper |\n"
"| `learn_mpo(marginals, N; ell, maxbond, sweeps)` | Sweep the one-site update of the paper along the chain, starting from the maximally mixed state |\n"
"| `max_fidelity(a, b)`, `mpo_overlap(a, b)` | Exact $\\mathcal{F}_{\\max}$ and $\\mathrm{tr}(ab)$ between two MPOs |\n"
"| `afc_estimate`, `afc_statistics`, `afc_prefix_curve` | AFC estimates of $\\mathcal{F}_{\\max}$ and $S_2$ from the testing settings, with jackknife error bars |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The learner stores the MPO in the Pauli basis. This keeps the model "
"Hermitian with unit trace, but it does not force the model to be positive "
"semidefinite, so the notebook reports how negative its eigenvalues become.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Small-scale simulator example {#small-scale-simulator-example}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Start with a system small enough to compute everything exactly: the depth-"
"one kicked-Ising state on eight qubits, with 12% local depolarizing noise to"
" model the hardware. Because the noisy state is known, you can compare the "
"learned model with it directly.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 1: Map classical inputs to a quantum problem {#step-1-map-classical-"
"inputs-to-a-quantum-problem}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The function `kicked_ising_reference` returns the exact state as an MPO. "
"With `depolarization=0.12`, the Bloch vector of every qubit shrinks by 12% "
"after the circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 2: Optimize problem for quantum hardware execution {#step-2-optimize-"
"problem-for-quantum-hardware-execution}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This example samples measurement outcomes classically, so there is no "
"circuit to transpile. The hardware example covers this step.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 3: Execute using Qiskit primitives {#step-3-execute-using-qiskit-"
"primitives}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Use `RandomMeas.jl` to draw 2,048 Haar-random measurement settings and "
"sample 256 shots from each, then split the settings evenly into training and"
" testing sets. Split by setting rather than by shot: shots from the same "
"setting share its rotations, so they are not independent.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 4: Post-process and return result in desired classical format "
"{#step-4-post-process-and-return-result-in-desired-classical-format}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Learn the model with the paper's depth-one parameters: windows that extend "
"$\\ell=2$ qubits on each side of the updated tensor, a bond dimension of 4, "
"and 20 sweeps starting from the maximally mixed state. The first sweep "
"updates neighboring pairs of tensors, which lets the bond dimension grow "
"from 1 to 4; every later sweep updates one tensor at a time at that fixed "
"bond dimension.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To reduce the statistical noise of the shadows, the paper uses *common "
"randomized measurements* (CRM) [\\[5\\]](#references). For each setting, it "
"subtracts the average shadow that a prior guess of the state would produce "
"in the same setting, and then adds back the prior's exact value. The "
"correction averages to zero, so the prior changes the size of the "
"statistical error but not what is estimated. Here the prior is the ideal "
"state with 10% local depolarization, close to the true 12%. On hardware, the"
" noise level is unknown, so the hardware example fits the prior to measured "
"purities instead, as the paper does.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/classical-"
"shadows/extracted-outputs/a5213240-0.svg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The residual measures how far the model's windows are from the training "
"shadows. It drops when the one-site updates start, in the second sweep, and "
"then stays approximately constant instead of reaching zero, because the "
"shadows contain statistical noise that a model with a small bond dimension "
"cannot reproduce.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Because this state is known, you can compare the learned model with it "
"exactly, and also compare the two simplest guesses with it: the CRM prior "
"and the ideal state.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The learned model is closer to the noisy state than either the prior or the "
"ideal state, so it has learned the noise from the data. Its small negative "
"eigenvalues come from the finite number of measurements.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"On hardware, the state is unknown, so you estimate $\\mathcal{F}_{\\max}$ "
"from the testing settings instead. AFC splits the chain into blocks "
"$A_1,\\dots,A_R$ of $k$ qubits and approximates the purity by\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\mathrm{tr}\\,\\rho^2\\approx\\frac{\\prod_{i=1}^{R-1}\\mathrm{tr}\\,\\rho_{A_iA_{i+1}}^2}{\\prod_{i=2}^{R-1}\\mathrm{tr}\\,\\rho_{A_i}^2},\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"and $\\mathrm{tr}(\\rho\\sigma)$ by the same ratio of local overlaps. With "
"$k=4$, the two blocks cover all eight qubits and the formula is exact; with "
"$k=2$, it relies on the factorization assumption. The jackknife error bars, "
"which leave out one testing setting at a time, cover the statistical error "
"of the testing data only, not the randomness of training or the AFC "
"approximation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Both held-out estimates agree with the exact value within their error "
"bars.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Large-scale hardware example {#large-scale-hardware-example}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now run the experiment on a 96-qubit chain of `ibm_fez`, a Heron r2 "
"processor. Like `ibm_brisbane`, it has a heavy-hex coupling map and a "
"similar noise level.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Collecting the data takes 1,024 Sampler jobs and about 34 minutes of QPU "
"time. If you don't have access to a QPU with at least 96 qubits, or the "
"queue is long, run the hardware example from the measurements recorded on "
"`ibm_fez` on 27 September 2026, which produced the results shown in this "
"tutorial. With `use_saved_data = true`, the notebook downloads these "
"measurements (15 MB) from [Zenodo](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24116174) "
"[\\[6\\]](#references) in Step 1 and skips job submission in Step 3. To "
"collect your own data, set it to `false`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 1: Map classical inputs to a quantum problem {#step-1-map-classical-"
"inputs-to-a-quantum-problem-1}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Each setting needs one circuit: the kicked-Ising circuit, a "
"$U(\\theta,\\phi,\\lambda)$ gate on each qubit for $u_i$, and a measurement "
"of every qubit. Sampling $\\theta=2\\arcsin\\sqrt{v}$, with $v$, "
"$\\phi/2\\pi$, and $\\lambda/2\\pi$ uniform in $[0,1)$, makes $U$ Haar "
"random. The `u3_matrix` function returns the matrix of $U$, which Step 4 "
"uses.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The budget is 1,024 settings of 1,024 shots, half the number of settings in "
"the paper, split evenly between training and testing. With saved data, the "
"cell loads the recorded angles. Otherwise, it draws new angles and saves "
"them before you submit anything, because you cannot analyze the outcomes "
"without them. If the file already exists, the cell loads it, so that the "
"angles still match the saved outcomes after you restart the session.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 2: Optimize problem for quantum hardware execution {#step-2-optimize-"
"problem-for-quantum-hardware-execution-1}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A chain is only as good as its worst qubit, so choose the 96 physical qubits"
" yourself. The `transpile` function of `Qiskit.jl` cannot yet accept a fixed"
" initial layout, and in a test on `ibm_phoenix`, the layout it chose "
"included a qubit with 51% readout error. The following chain is the 96-qubit"
" path in the `ibm_fez` coupling map that the error-aware layout of the "
"Python Qiskit `transpile` function (with `optimization_level=3`) selected "
"from the calibration data of 27 September 2026. Its mean readout error is "
"1.6%, but a heavy-hex lattice has few 96-qubit paths, and this one includes "
"qubit 35, which has a 15% readout error. Calibrations change, so choose a "
"new chain from the current [calibration "
"data](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/computers) before you run on another day"
" or QPU.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To keep this chain, write the circuits directly in native gates instead of "
"transpiling them. A $U(\\theta,\\phi,\\lambda)$ gate equals "
"$R_Z(\\phi+\\pi)\\,\\sqrt{X}\\,R_Z(\\theta+\\pi)\\,\\sqrt{X}\\,R_Z(\\lambda)$"
" up to a global phase. This decomposition covers both the random rotations "
"and the kick $e^{-i\\frac{\\pi}{8}X}=U(\\pi/4,-\\pi/2,\\pi/2)$. The gate "
"$e^{i\\frac{\\pi}{4}Z_jZ_{j+1}}$ equals a CZ gate followed by $S^\\dagger$ "
"on both qubits, up to a global phase. Because these $S^\\dagger$ gates are "
"diagonal, they commute with the CZ gates, so each qubit collects one "
"$R_Z(-\\pi/2)$ per bond before its measurement rotation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 3: Execute using Qiskit primitives {#step-3-execute-using-qiskit-"
"primitives-1}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`QiskitIBMRuntime.jl` runs one circuit per Sampler job, so collecting the "
"data requires 1,024 jobs. The following cell keeps up to 32 jobs in the "
"queue and saves the outcomes of each setting as soon as its job finishes. "
"Each shot is returned as a vector of bits, in which element $i$ is the "
"outcome of chain qubit $i$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You must retrieve the results in the same session that submitted the jobs, "
"because `QiskitIBMRuntime.jl` cannot yet look up a job by its ID ([qiskit-"
"ibm-runtime-c issue #28](https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit-ibm-"
"runtime-c/issues/28)). If a job fails, the cell issues a warning and "
"continues. If the session stops, run Steps 1 through 3 again. Rerunning the "
"cell submits only the settings whose outcomes are missing. With "
"`use_saved_data = true`, the cell submits nothing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 4: Post-process and return result in desired classical format "
"{#step-4-post-process-and-return-result-in-desired-classical-format-1}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This step reads only saved data: the downloaded `ibm_fez` run if "
"`use_saved_data` is true, or otherwise the files that Step 3 wrote. You can "
"therefore run it in a new session without submitting jobs (run the Setup "
"cell, the `use_saved_data` cell, and Step 1 first). The downloaded file "
"stores the 96 outcomes of each shot packed into 12 bytes, most significant "
"bit first, so the loader unpacks them. `RandomMeas.jl` labels outcomes 1 and"
" 2, so the loader also adds 1 to every bit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Before learning, check the data. A broken qubit or a badly calibrated gate "
"makes the measured state much more mixed around it, and it can make the "
"learner diverge. The purity of a few neighboring qubits is a model-free "
"benchmark of this noise: the function `get_purity` from `RandomMeas.jl` "
"estimates $\\mathrm{tr}\\,\\rho_A^2$ for a subsystem $A$ by comparing pairs "
"of shots from the same setting. Compute the Rényi-2 entropy "
"$S_2=-\\log_2\\mathrm{tr}\\,\\rho_A^2$ of every window of six neighboring "
"qubits, and compare it with the same window of the ideal state. The ideal "
"value is not zero, because each window is entangled with the rest of the "
"chain; noise increases it, and a broken qubit pushes every window that "
"contains it toward the maximum of six.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/classical-"
"shadows/extracted-outputs/b38405b1-2.svg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Every window is more mixed than the ideal state, by about half a bit to one "
"bit, and the noisiest stretch is around chain positions 30 to 35. No window "
"comes close to the maximum of six, so no qubit is broken, and the analysis "
"uses the whole 96-qubit chain. If some windows had an entropy close to six, "
"you would remove the qubit that those windows share and learn the longest "
"part of the chain without it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The same purities determine the CRM prior. For each window, `fitted_prior` "
"takes the ideal state and fits a local depolarizing strength so that its "
"purity matches the purity measured in the training settings, as in the "
"paper.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The `learn_and_validate` function in the following cell performs the rest of"
" the analysis. It learns the model from the training settings, then "
"estimates $\\mathcal{F}_{\\max}$ and $S_2$ on the testing settings for the "
"first $N$ qubits of the chain, in steps of 12. These are the quantities in "
"Figure 1(c,d) of the paper. The ideal state is evaluated on the same testing"
" data as a baseline.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The cell runs the function with $\\ell=2$ and then repeats it with $\\ell=1$"
" and $\\ell=0$ to see how much the size of the learning windows matters. The"
" bond dimension can be at most $4^\\ell$, so $\\ell=0$ learns a product "
"state: each qubit on its own, with no correlations between qubits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/classical-"
"shadows/extracted-outputs/7b1b7337-2.svg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The results reproduce both claims of the paper on a different processor:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**The model describes the data far better than the ideal state.** At $N=96$,"
" the held-out $\\mathcal{F}_{\\max}$ of the $\\ell=2$ model is 0.59 ± 0.10, "
"compared with 0.0035 for the ideal state. The paper reports about 0.75 on "
"`ibm_brisbane`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**The entropy of the model agrees with the testing data at every $N$.** At "
"$N=96$, it is 10.2 from the model and 10.0 ± 0.3 from the testing data, or "
"$S_2/N\\approx 0.10$, compared with 0.14 to 0.15 on the older "
"`ibm_brisbane`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The comparison of window sizes shows where the information is. With "
"$\\ell=1$, the model is almost as good "
"($\\mathcal{F}_{\\max}=0.55\\pm0.09$), so for these data, correlations "
"beyond a few qubits add little. With $\\ell=0$, the product state misses the"
" entanglement between neighboring qubits: its entropy grows to 32 instead of"
" 10, and its $\\mathcal{F}_{\\max}$ falls even faster than that of the ideal"
" state, to $6\\times10^{-6}$ at $N=96$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Keep the following limitations in mind when you read these numbers:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Training split.** The error bars cover the statistical error of the "
"testing data only. Other random splits of the same settings give "
"$\\mathcal{F}_{\\max}$ between 0.49 and 0.59 at $N=96$ for $\\ell=2$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Final sweep.** The learner always returns the model from the final sweep, "
"so the testing data never influence the model.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Positivity.** The model is not constrained to be positive semidefinite; "
"its most negative eigenvalue in a learning window is about $-0.05$. Its "
"$\\mathcal{F}_{\\max}$ and $S_2$ therefore describe an operator, not "
"certified properties of a physical state.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Factorization.** Every held-out estimate relies on the AFC assumption, "
"which the measurements themselves cannot certify.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Next steps {#next-steps}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<Admonition type=\"tip\" title=\"Recommendations\">\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Repeat the experiment at depth two with the paper's parameters for that "
"depth ($\\ell=3$ and a maximum bond dimension of 8), and determine how many "
"measurement settings the learner needs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Learn with the two-site update instead (`update=:two_site` in "
"`training_marginals` and `learn_mpo`), which truncates the bond dimension "
"after every step, and see how it behaves with fewer training settings.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Learn without the CRM prior (`prior=nothing`) and compare the held-out "
"fidelity.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Apply the same analysis to the authors' published [`ibm_brisbane` "
"measurements](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18323569).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Read the [supplement of the paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.12550) for the"
" derivation of the update and of the AFC estimators.\n"
msgstr ""

